{"name":"Black Marlin","hex":"#383740","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"marlin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any species of game fish belonging to either of the genera Tetrapturus or Makaira, with a crested dorsal fin and a pointed, spear-like projection of the upper jaw."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/marlin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black marlin","blocks":[{"text":"The black marlin is a species of marlin found in tropical and subtropical areas of the Indian and Pacific Oceans approximately between 40 degrees North and 45 degrees South, while in some instances venturing into more temperate waters. Reaching lengths of over 4.5 m (15 ft), it is one of the largest marlins and also one of the largest bony fish. Marlins are among the fastest fish, but speeds are often exaggerated in popular media, such as reports of 129 km/h (80 mph). A 2016 study estimated maximum swimming speeds from muscle contraction times, which in turn limit the tail-beat frequency; the study suggested a theoretical upper limit for the black marlin's burst speed of 36 km/h (22 mph)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Istiompax_indica.jpg/330px-Istiompax_indica.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_marlin","wikidata":"Q998315"},{"title":"Marlin","blocks":[{"text":"Marlins are fish from the family Istiophoridae, which includes between 9 and 11 species, depending on the taxonomic authority."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Atlantic_blue_marlin.jpg/330px-Atlantic_blue_marlin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marlin","wikidata":"Q123478185"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Black_Marlin_Artwork_by_Frank_Olsen.jpg/500px-Black_Marlin_Artwork_by_Frank_Olsen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Black_Marlin_Artwork_by_Frank_Olsen.jpg/500px-Black_Marlin_Artwork_by_Frank_Olsen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image of a black marlin done by Frank Olsen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Marlin_Artwork_by_Frank_Olsen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Marlin_Types.jpg/500px-Marlin_Types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Marlin_Types.jpg/500px-Marlin_Types.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A 